From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius DKM <mariusdkm@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:37:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add a blockaudio backend that writes timed blocks of audio to
 a pipe

The blockaudio backend writes the audio to a named pipe as a sequence of records. Each record is
a 40-byte header followed by a block of audio. The header carries a version, its own length, the
payload length, a sequence number, a CRC-32 of the header, flags (timed, flush, stop), the
encoded output format, the RTP timestamp of the first frame and the time at which it should be
played. Later versions only append fields, so older readers can skip what they don't know.

This lets a reader that schedules the audio itself play it in sync with the sender's other
AirPlay receivers, and see every change of format as it happens. The format is documented in
"ADVANCED TOPICS/BlockAudio.md".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
