+# Block Audio Output
+
+The `blockaudio` backend writes the audio to a named pipe, like the `pipe` backend, but in
+records: each block of audio is preceded by a header that gives its format, its RTP timestamp and
+the time at which its first frame should be played. A reader can then schedule the audio itself,
+for example to keep its own outputs in sync with the source's other AirPlay speakers, and it sees
+every change of format as it happens.
+
+Build Shairport Sync with `--with-blockaudio`, then select the backend and, optionally, the pipe:
+
+```
+general =
+{
+	output_backend = "blockaudio";
+};
+
+blockaudio =
+{
+	name = "/tmp/shairport-sync-blockaudio"; // this is the default
+};
+```
+
+The `output_rate`, `output_format` and `output_channels` settings work as for the `pipe` backend.
+
+## Records
+
+The pipe carries a sequence of records. Each record is a header, followed by `payload_length`
+bytes of audio. Header-only records have no audio.
+
+Header fields are in the host's native byte order. The play time is only meaningful on the same
+machine anyway (see [Timing](#timing)).
+
+| Offset | Size | Field | Description |
+| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |
+| 0 | 4 | `magic` | The bytes `SSBA`. Written as bytes, so the same on any host. |
+| 4 | 2 | `version` | `1`. |
+| 6 | 2 | `header_length` | Length of the header in bytes: `40` in version 1. |
+| 8 | 4 | `payload_length` | Length of the audio that follows, in bytes. `0` for header-only records. |
+| 12 | 4 | `sequence` | One more than in the record before, including header-only records. Wraps around. |
+| 16 | 4 | `header_crc32` | CRC-32 of the first `header_length` bytes, computed with this field set to `0`. |
+| 20 | 2 | `flags` | See below. |
+| 22 | 2 | reserved | `0`. |
+| 24 | 4 | `format` | Format of the audio, encoded as below. |
+| 28 | 4 | `rtp` | RTP timestamp of the first frame, at the source's rate. `0` if not timed. |
+| 32 | 8 | `play_time` | Time at which the first frame should be played, in nanoseconds. `0` if not timed. |
+
+The CRC-32 is the one used by zlib, Ethernet and PNG (reflected polynomial `0xEDB88320`), so it can
+be checked with, e.g., Python's `zlib.crc32()`.
+
+The flags are:
+
+| Bit | Name | Meaning |
+| ---: | :--- | :--- |
+| 0 | `timed` | `rtp` and `play_time` are valid. Records that are not timed are silence inserted by Shairport Sync. |
+| 1 | `flush` | Discard any audio received but not yet played, e.g. after the source seeks or pauses. |
+| 2 | `stop` | The play session has ended. |
+
+Other bits are reserved and are `0`. Flush and stop are sent in header-only records, and both may
+be set in the same record.
+
+### Format
+
+`format` is Shairport Sync's encoded output format:
+
+| Bits | Content |
+| :--- | :--- |
+| 0–5 | Sample format, see below. |
+| 6–24 | Frames per second, divided by 2. |
+| 25–31 | Number of channels. |
+
+The audio is interleaved. The sample formats are:
+
+| Value | Format | Bytes per sample |
+| ---: | :--- | ---: |
+| 1 | `S8` | 1 |
+| 2 | `U8` | 1 |
+| 3 | `S16_LE` | 2 |
+| 4 | `S16_BE` | 2 |
+| 5 | `S24_LE` (in 32 bits) | 4 |
+| 6 | `S24_BE` (in 32 bits) | 4 |
+| 7 | `S24_3LE` | 3 |
+| 8 | `S24_3BE` | 3 |
+| 9 | `S32_LE` | 4 |
+| 10 | `S32_BE` | 4 |
+
+Header-only records carry the current format, or `0` if no format has been set yet.
+
+### Later Versions
+
+Later versions only append fields after the ones above, which keep their offsets and meanings. A
+reader that knows version 1 can read any later version: it uses the fields it knows and skips the
+rest of the header using `header_length`.
+
+## Timing
+
+The frames of each record follow on directly from those of the record before, so frame `n` of a
+timed record should be played at `play_time + n × 1,000,000,000 / rate` nanoseconds. A record that
+is not timed plays straight after the record before it.
+
+The play time is taken from the clock Shairport Sync uses internally: `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` (or
+`CLOCK_MONOTONIC` if that is not available) on Linux and the BSDs, and `mach_absolute_time()` on
+macOS. A reader must compare it with the same clock, so it has to run on the same machine (or in a
+container on the same kernel).
+
+The RTP timestamp can be matched against the RTP timestamps in the [metadata](Metadata.md), e.g. to
+know which track a block belongs to.
+
+## Reading the Pipe
+
+* **Keep reading.** Read the pipe continuously from start-up, whether or not anything is playing.
+  While the pipe is full, Shairport Sync waits to write the next record, which holds up the
+  player, as with the `pipe` backend.
+* **Flush and stop can arrive late.** Shairport Sync never waits to write them. If a record is
+  being written at that moment, the marker follows straight after it. If the pipe is full, it is
+  written before the next record instead, so a reader that keeps the pipe drained gets it at once.
+  A late flush and stop may arrive together in one record.
+* **End of file.** If a write fails, or the play session is torn down while a record is being
+  written, Shairport Sync closes its end of the pipe instead of finishing the record, and reopens
+  it when the next block of audio is ready. A reader that gets end-of-file should discard any
+  partial record and treat it as a flush, then open the pipe again.
+* **Resynchronising.** A reader that starts reading a pipe that already has data in it, e.g. after
+  a restart, may start in the middle of a record. To find the start of the next one, search for
+  `SSBA` and check `header_crc32`. Optionally, check that the next record begins exactly
+  `header_length + payload_length` bytes later. Audio is full of arbitrary bytes, so don't trust a
+  header whose CRC doesn't match. Gaps can be detected from `sequence`.
+
+### Example
+
+A minimal reader in Python:
+
+```python
+import struct, zlib
+
+HEADER = struct.Struct("=4sHHIIIHHIIQ")  # native byte order, no padding
+
+def read_exactly(pipe, n):
+    data = pipe.read(n)
+    if len(data) < n:
+        raise EOFError  # discard the partial record, treat it as a flush, reopen the pipe
+    return data
+
+with open("/tmp/shairport-sync-blockaudio", "rb") as pipe:
+    while True:
+        fixed = read_exactly(pipe, HEADER.size)
+        (magic, version, header_length, payload_length, sequence, crc,
+         flags, _, fmt, rtp, play_time) = HEADER.unpack(fixed)
+        if magic != b"SSBA" or header_length < HEADER.size:
+            raise ValueError("lost sync: search for the next SSBA")  # see above
+        header = bytearray(fixed + read_exactly(pipe, header_length - HEADER.size))
+        header[16:20] = bytes(4)
+        if zlib.crc32(header) != crc:
+            raise ValueError("lost sync: search for the next SSBA")
+        audio = read_exactly(pipe, payload_length)
+        channels, rate, sample_format = fmt >> 25, ((fmt >> 6) & 0x7FFFF) * 2, fmt & 0x3F
+        # ... schedule `audio` to start at `play_time` if flags & 1, handle flush/stop ...
+```
